Texinfo manuals translations
============================

A po4a texinfo parser based on the Texinfo SWIG interface is to be
integrated in po4a, maybe for the next Texinfo release, which
should also have the SWIG interface built by default if SWIG is detected.

Using po4a with one of the two po4a texinfo parsers could be the
recommended way to translate manuals, solving some of the issues discussed
in the past.

Discussions started post 7.3 release:
https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/bug-texinfo/2026-07/msg00050.html
https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/bug-texinfo/2026-07/msg00051.html
https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/bug-texinfo/2026-07/msg00052.html

Manual name:

The recommendation for translated manual Texinfo name is to use the
untranslated manual name and postpend a language code LL taking into account
@documentlanguage, @documentscript and @documentlanguagevariants, with
a -, for example between the untranslated manual name and the LL postfix, for
example manualname-LL.texi.  That way it is easy to make cross-references to
the translated manual name and register independent information in htmlxref for
cross-references to external translated HTML manuals.

Anchors and node names in translated manuals:

When starting from an untranslated manual, nodes, anchor and float names
(hereafter called targets) would be in the untranslated language (English).
Then the targets and the associated commands should be translated as the
manual is translated.  In the short term, it is recommended to keep
anchors with the untranslated target names such that cross-references
from external manuals can be either to translated or untranslated target
names.  In the longer term, it is recommended to keep a mention of the
untranslated targets, such that it is possible for persons not
understanding the translation language to move sections around, but
explicit anchors could be removed, and it is even recommended to remove
them in the long term.

Example of macros that could help the transition and remain in the final
translated manual.  "name" would be the translated name, and "en" the
untranslated name.

@c to be used to have an anchor added, for the short term
@macro nodetranslatedanchor {name, en}
@node \name\
@anchor{\en\}
@end macro

@c to be used to have a mention to the untranslated node, but no anchor
@c for the long term
@macro nodetranslatedmention {name, en}
@node \name\
@end macro

@c use in document
@nodetranslatedanchor{Mon noeud, My node}

